speeches.en:
Resolution submitted to ascertain the views of the Members of the People's Majlis on the matter of Public Service Media discriminating between political parties in carrying out the responsibilities placed on it, not giving the Maldivian Democratic Party the opportunity to provide information to the Maldivian people, and failing to ensure the editorial independence set out in law. Preamble: This is a resolution submitted under Article 189 of the Rules of Procedure of the People's Majlis to ascertain the views of the Members of the People's Majlis on the matter of Public Service Media, in carrying out the obligations placed on it in its capacity as public broadcaster under Law No. 9/2015 (Public Service Media Act) and Law No. 11/2008 (General Elections Act), discriminating [between parties], not giving the Maldivian Democratic Party the opportunity to provide information to the Maldivian people, giving the opportunity to the party the government belongs to, and completely stripping away the editorial independence set out in law. Resolution: Whereas the purpose of Public Service Media is to remain a national media serving all regions of the Maldives, within the general principles and policies set out in law, free from influence and with editorial independence; whereas Article 5(k) of Law No. 9/2015 (Public Service Media Act) sets out, as policies of the state media, working to continue providing the services of Public Service Media without any discrimination among the citizens, and working to establish equality also in giving opportunities to individuals and to various groups through Public Service Media; and whereas, in addition, Article 5 states that working to build a well-informed and aware citizenry in religious, economic, social, political and all other fields useful to the nation, and informing the citizens of the news, information and developments on various international matters important to the Maldivian people and on various national matters, are duties imposed on the state media; whereas Article 6 of Law No. 9/2015 (Public Service Media Act) states that all parties must work to keep Public Service Media as an institution free from influence and with full editorial independence, and that no party holding power or status, whether political, financial, administrative or otherwise, may attempt to exert influence over Public Service Media, either directly or indirectly; whereas Law No.

11/2008 (General Elections Act), Article 30(a), states that from the day the Elections Commission officially announces the names of the candidates contesting in elections until 6 in the evening of the day before polling, all parties broadcasting in the Maldives must allocate time, in accordance with this Act and regulations made under it, for broadcasting advertisements and programmes by the candidates and political parties contesting that election to seek support; and whereas paragraph (b) of that Article states that broadcasters must give the time allocated under this Article to candidates, or to the political parties the candidates belong to, with equality; and whereas, although Article 4 of the Guidelines on Broadcasting Political Content during Elections, formulated to achieve these purposes, states that the party with the greatest responsibility for broadcasting voter awareness programmes, maintaining an equal principle in giving the time allocated to candidates, and providing true information fairly without siding with any party in presenting news is the public service broadcaster, and that it must work giving special importance to this; yet, with no regard for the laws and principles referred to above, instead of working as a national or state media, the state media channels have in the recent past worked by giving opportunity to a particular group, entirely removing the editorial independence set out in law, and departing from equality and fairness in order to protect the political or other personal interests of a particular party; and whereas, even after the conclusion of the public referendum to determine whether the people consent to holding the presidential election and the People's Majlis election together, the Maldivian Democratic Party has not been given the opportunity to provide the people with full information on the matter to an adequate extent; whereas the state broadcaster Public Service Media published news claiming that the ruling party won the most votes in the local council elections and the elections for Women's Development Committees held on 4 April 2026, deliberately providing incorrect information to the general public; whereas Maldivian newspapers reported that before the election scheduled for 4 April 2026, 2 people were promoted to the post of executive director of the state broadcaster, a further 3 people were promoted to the post of deputy director, and a former assistant manager was promoted to lead the TV production department;

now therefore, in order to carry out the matters that must be done in fulfilment of the constitutional duty placed on the People's Majlis to oversee whether the matters carried out by the executive are being done or not and to hold it accountable, as set out in Article 70(b)(3) of the Constitution of the Republic of Maldives, I submit this resolution to ascertain the views of the Members of the People's Majlis on this matter. Thank you.
